WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.419
Film Courage: I think you said earlier that
documentaries are kind of…this is their

00:00:03.419 --> 00:00:04.419
time right now.

00:00:04.419 --> 00:00:05.419
Why do you think now so?

00:00:05.419 --> 00:00:10.200
Matthew Miele, filmmaker [Always At The Carlyle]:
I think because with the streaming that is

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.030
happening, they are able to get out their
to a bigger audience and there is also this

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:20.290
notion of a doc series that’s been popping
up and is very popular now where you can tell

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:27.480
longer-form things like HBO is doing things
that stretch over a few nights.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.899
Netflix and other streamers are doing things
calling doc-series where it’s four to six

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:35.739
episodes of certain subjects.

00:00:35.739 --> 00:00:42.079
But I do think that reality these days is
somewhat more interesting than what fiction

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:43.079
can even dream up.

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:47.800
I mean there are so many cool things happening
in the world you kind of have to put a magnifying

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:53.429
glass on it to examine it and to me we are
living in such an amazing time where there

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:57.859
are all these compelling things everyday.

00:00:57.859 --> 00:01:02.750
And there’s a certain thing where you don’t
really want to chase a headline because it

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.100
will be irrelevant tomorrow.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.310
But if you look at certain things that are
hit upon sometimes like The Carlyle Hotel

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:15.310
where you continue to see stories happen there,
where you continue to see history unfold,

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:17.079
those are the kind of things you want to grasp
onto.

00:01:17.079 --> 00:01:22.930
For me, they are not only relevant for historical
reasons but they are constantly current.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:28.770
So that’s why I think for a film like this,
something will happen with the streamers and

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:34.789
how accessible it will be to everyone eventually
besides the pockets that the theaters are

00:01:34.789 --> 00:01:39.729
appealing to audience-wise because I just
feel like it’s always going to be in the

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:43.939
news in sense, they are always going to be
something that occurs there and that’s just

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:44.939
the magic of the place.

00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:47.789
So that’s what I feel like a film like this
[Matthew’s latest documentary film ALWAYS

00:01:47.789 --> 00:01:50.450
AT THE CARLYLE] will appeal and be popular.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.229
Similar with Bergdorf’s, that happened.

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:58.550
I think is this fashion hunger out there,
people just can’t get enough of it.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:02.479
And when that film came out in 2015 I think
it was at the height (I think it’s fallen

00:02:02.479 --> 00:02:03.659
off a little bit).

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:09.450
I think people are kind of getting over-saturated
it.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:13.780
But I don’t know docs to me I mean they
are selling, so I know there is a marketplace

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.520
for them and they are selling at a decent
enough level that you can continue making

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.520
them.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:23.250
And I think that’s exciting for any filmmaker
to just continue telling your story and if

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:27.370
you have bidders on your content, you can’t
be in a better spot because you continue to

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:28.370
do what you do.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:35.890
So I feel like that golden period, you can’t
really recognize it when it’s happening

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:42.490
but I think when we look back on this decade…I
would say from 2010 to 2020 we’ll be like

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:43.490
“Wow!

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:46.100
That was an amazing moment where we were telling
amazing stories.”

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.930
Film Courage: Plus we came out of a recession
(or supposedly we’ve come out of one) so

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:56.150
during that time and so it’s a relatively
inexpensive way to get a film made if you’re

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.150
doing it in piecemeal.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.922
Matthew: It definitely is and what’s so
interesting about The Recession is so THE

00:02:59.922 --> 00:03:03.210
BIG SHORT comes out and it kind of examines
what happened there.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.780
And I love filmmaking like that, too.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:10.780
It’s like the biopics and the journalistic
studies of things like ALL THE PRESIDENT’S

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:14.680
MEN and SPOTLIGHT, like those to me (if you
do them right) they feel like a documentary

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:15.680
almost.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.960
So I feel like we’re in that mode of storytelling
right now more than ever where even Hollywood

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.820
is on to the notion of let’s tell real stories,
let’s tell what’s happening.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:32.960
But the balance is of course that you’re
in the Marvel world…I feel like the indie

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.230
scene and the ones that are the more renegade
pictures are the ones telling the real stories

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:42.950
but they’re balanced well with this whole
Marvel universe where it’s this imaginative

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:48.090
fiction and sci fi etcetera where we are really
getting a great blend.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:52.730
And I feel like (as I said) when we look back
on it we’ll be like “Wow, that was a really

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:53.730
great moment.”

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:57.730
A lot of people bemoan that the seventies
are over and that storytelling is not happening

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:59.460
any more.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:04.780
I think it was ’66 to ’76 people look
at and the eighties had all those comedies

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:05.780
right?

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:10.760
But I don’t know, I feel like right now
we’re having a really great moment and we’ll

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.900
look back on it and say “Wow, that was terrific
to be a part of.”

